    <!-- Accessibility disclosure, required by Google Play. Mirrored in docs/play-store/disclosure.md. -->
    <string name="disclosure_title">About the Accessibility service</string>
    <string name="disclosure_intro">NavGesture needs Android\'s Accessibility service to turn edge swipes into Back, Home and Recents. Before you turn it on, here is exactly what it does.</string>
    <string name="disclosure_reads_title">What it reads</string>
    <string name="disclosure_reads_body">Only where and when you touch inside thin strips along the left, right and bottom edges of the screen.</string>
    <string name="disclosure_does_title">What it does with it</string>
    <string name="disclosure_does_body">It decides whether the touch is a gesture, then performs Back, Home, Recents (Android\'s recent-apps screen), opens NavGesture\'s own app row, or starts Circle to Search. A touch that is not a gesture is passed on to the app underneath, at the same spot.</string>
    <string name="disclosure_never_title">What it never does</string>
    <string name="disclosure_never_body">It cannot see what is on your screen or what you type. It does not store your touches, and it does not send them anywhere.</string>
    <string name="disclosure_agree">Agree</string>
    <string name="disclosure_not_now">Not now</string>
    <string name="disclosure_footnote">Agree opens Android\'s Accessibility settings, where you switch NavGesture on. You can switch it off there at any time.</string>
    <string name="usage_access_explainer">With Usage access, NavGesture reads which apps you opened recently, only to list them in the app row. The list stays on this phone.</string>
